= Overfitting =

== Overview ==
'''Overfitting''' occurs when a learning procedure adapts too strongly to finite-sample details, noise, artifacts, or the development process so that apparent fit is better than performance on genuinely unseen target data.

Overfitting is about generalization, not model size alone.

== Training and evaluation ==
A typical overfit model has lower training error than test error.

However, the size of the gap depends on the metric, data, model, regularization, and evaluation process.

Repeated tuning on a validation set can overfit that validation set even when model parameters are never directly trained on it.

== Model capacity ==
High model capacity can increase the ability to fit noise, but:

<pre>
large model ≠ overfit model
</pre>

Modern overparameterized models can interpolate training data and still generalize well.

This is one reason the classical single U-shaped complexity story is not universal.

== Memorization ==
Memorization can contribute to overfitting, but it is not identical to overfitting.

A model can memorize rare or exceptional examples and still generalize useful structure to new data.

== Prevention and diagnosis ==
Common tools include:

* held-out validation;
* cross-validation;
* regularization;
* early stopping;
* data augmentation;
* larger or better-matched datasets;
* leakage checks;
* independent testing.

No single method guarantees generalization.

== Related concepts ==
[[Generalization (machine learning)]], [[Validation set]], [[Test set]], [[Bias–variance tradeoff]], [[Model selection bias]], and [[Data leakage (machine learning)]] distinguish different forms of apparent performance failure.

